Here's the full CartComponent with the UI/JSX part:

```jsx
import React from'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from'react-redux';
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice';
import { Link } from'react-router-dom';

const CartComponent = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const cart = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const total = cart.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price * i.quantity, 0);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Shopping Cart</h1>
      <ul>
        {cart.items.map(i => (
          <li key={i.id}>
            {i.name}
            <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(i.id))}>
              x
            </button>
            <select
              value={i.quantity}
              onChange={e =>
                dispatch(
                  addItem({
                    id: i.id,
                    name: i.name,
                    price: i.price,
                    quantity: Number(e.target.value)
                  })
                )
              }
            >
              {[...Array(10)].map((_, i) => (
                <option key={i} value={i + 1}>
                  {i + 1}
                </option>
              ))}
            </select>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>Total: ${total}</p>
      <button>
        <Link to="/checkout">Proceed to Checkout</Link>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default CartComponent;
```

Note: This assumes the cart slice includes item objects with `id`, `name`, and `price` properties, and that `removeItem` action takes the `id` of the item to remove. You'll need to adjust that based on your app's specific store shape.

Here's a basic one:

```jsx
import React from'react'
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from'react-redux'
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice'
import './Cart.css'

const Item = ({ item, remove }) => (
  <div className="cart-item">
    <h3>{item.name}</h3>
    <p>Price: {item.price}</p>
    <button onClick={() => remove(item.id)}>Remove</button>
  </div>
)

const Cart = () => {
  const items = useSelector(state => state.cart.items)
  const dispatch = useDispatch()

  const handleAdd = (item) => dispatch(addItem(item))

  return (
    <div className="cart">
      <h1>Cart</h1>
      {items.map(item => (
        <Item
          key={item.id}
          item={item}
          remove={dispatch(removeItem)}
        />
      ))}
      <button
        className="cart-add"
        onClick={() => {
          const newItem = { name: 'New Item', price: 10.99 };
          handleAdd(newItem)
        }}
      >
        Add new item
      </button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default Cart
```

Note the global state `items` is a flat array. The dispatch function for `removeItem` is passed directly to the `Item` component for use in its remove button, since it depends on both the component and its props. The `handleAdd` function wraps `dispatch(addItem)` to keep the dispatching wrapped in a function higher up in the tree. The button at the bottom is a way to add a new item without having to interact with a 'products' component.

